1595	## Inspection and execution
1596	
1597	`getStopReason()` reports the stop cause; every runner also has synchronous `inspect()`. For tree/DAG runners, inspection returns a detached, frozen snapshot with status, traversal kind, source mode, execution mode, effective concurrency, ready visits, frames, visitor chains, pending callback identities, event boundaries, physical in-flight callback count, and buffered event count. Inspection never calls user code, consumes an event, or advances traversal, and snapshots contain reference ids rather than callback functions, promises, or vertex data. Pending boundary and buffered event counts include recorded observations regardless of the current delivery selection; they describe separate kernel/session queues and should not be summed as unique observations.
1598	
1599	`TreeDagExecutionInspection<Phase>` retains the concrete runner's callback phase type. `readyVisits[].phase` and `chains[].phase` name callback phases; chain `state` is `running | paused | done | invalid`, and `visitorIndex` identifies its current position. Each chain retains its admitted `executionOptions` and exposes `commandCommitMode`. The chain snapshot’s `visitorPhases` governs that chain; its `emitEvents` is historical configuration only. Top-level `executionOptions.emitEvents` governs current delivery, including events committed by older chains. Pending requests use `operation: resolveRoot | resolveChild | resolvePrerequisite | transformChildHints | getVertexIdFromHint | visit`. Frame `stage`, detached owner tokens, `waitingFor`, and request validity describe execution mechanics; owner tokens are runner-local identities, not vertex IDs. Walking likewise uses callback `phase`, `visitorIndex`, and pending request `operation` with its own operation vocabulary.
1600	
1601	Resolved graph vertices expose `visitStage: ResolvedVisitStage`; query it with `getVisitStageOf(ref)`. Stages are `DISCOVERED`, `INITIAL_VISIT_ELIGIBLE`, `INITIAL_VISIT_STARTED`, `INITIAL_VISIT_COMMITTED`, `COMPLETION_VISIT_ELIGIBLE`, and `COMPLETE`. These preserve each family's scheduling boundaries: tree initial callbacks can still be `INITIAL_VISIT_ELIGIBLE` while DAG initial callbacks are `INITIAL_VISIT_STARTED`. A stage does not establish callback activity. Deferred DAG work can remain `INITIAL_VISIT_STARTED` after all callbacks drain; completion eligibility precedes completion callbacks and committed C. Use inspection's in-flight count/chains for callback activity and DAG `summary()` / milestone queries for W/C facts, independently of visit stages. Original graph views retain structural queries without visit stages; runner `getStatus()` remains the separate execution lifecycle.
