1690	## Views, handles, identity and paths
1691	
1692	<a id="safe-tree-queries"></a>
1693	
1694	Use `runner.getResolvedTreeView()` or a standalone store’s `asReadOnly()` for tree queries. `ResolvedTreeView<T>` has `getRoot`, `has`, `getChildrenOf`, `getParentOf`, `requirePathTo`, and `getResolutionContextOf`. `ResolvedGraphView<T, ID>` is available through `getResolvedGraphView()`. Both are stable live query capabilities with frozen detached collections and restricted vertex handles. DAG runners return the tree facade in tree-source mode and null in graph-source mode. Ordinary runners have no `getResolvedTreeStore()` method; edit through visitor commands or construct a standalone `ResolvedTreeStore` for advanced editing.
1695	
1696	`VertexHandle<T>` exposes `getRefId()` and `getValue()` without editing authority. `getValue()` returns a frozen `VertexView<T>` with readonly `$data` and `$childHints` fields, query methods, and detached frozen hint arrays. The handle is canonical across callbacks, source contexts, events, and query views; its value remains live. An event’s `vertex` wraps the value captured for that event, so later commands can change its live `vertexRef` without changing the recorded vertex. `clone()` creates a separate editable `Vertex` and hint collection. Opaque caller data, IDs, and individual hint payloads retain identity and are not frozen. `MutableRef<T>` and mutable `Vertex<T>` remain standalone advanced editing capabilities. A `TreeVertexRecord` stores `metadata` and structural `children`, separate from the Vertex payload/hint fields `$data` and `$childHints`. Graph wrappers use `ResolvedGraphVertex`, `ResolvedVisitStage`, and `StructuralChildEdge`.
1697	
1698	Tree callback and source contexts expose `resolvedTreeView`; graph contexts expose `resolvedGraphView`. Neither exposes a live mutable store. Source resolution metadata is a stable frozen projection. For a synthetic DAG entry slot, `parentVertex` and `parentVertexRef` are both null; ordinary graph parents and tree parents are non-null restricted facets. Entry-slot `depth`, `hintIndex`, and `vertexHint` retain their discovery meanings. Original tree and graph views remain in the input type domain.
1699	
1700	`MutableRef.getRefId()` is the generated reference UUID. `ResolvedGraphView.requireVertexId(ref)` returns the host's arbitrary vertex ID and `getVertexRefById(id)` returns its reference. Live and original graph views use the same names. Retired `getId`, `getIdOf`, and `getVertexById` aliases are removed. Do not substitute the reference UUID for a host ID or stringify object/symbol/undefined IDs.
1701	
1702	`ResolvedTreeStore.requirePathTo(ref, { noRoot?, noSelf? })` returns the resolved ancestry in root-to-vertex order and throws if the reference is absent from that tree. Original-tree paths use the retained view’s own references. Deleting a resolved vertex removes its resolved descendants and detaches it from its parent; deleting the root clears the root pointer.
1703	
1704	<a id="explicit-input-ownership-and-query-names"></a>
1705	
1706	`VertexContentInput.$childHints` and `REWRITE_CHILD_HINTS.newHints` accept readonly collections. A standalone `Vertex` copies hint membership at construction and for every `clone()` path; individual hint elements and `$data` retain caller identity. `getChildHints()` returns the owned editable standalone collection, while observed `VertexView` collections remain frozen and detached.
1707	
1708	`isChildExpansionSkipped(ref)` answers whether DFS explicitly skipped expansion; it is independent of hint emptiness and structural leaf status. `requireVertexId`, `requirePathsTo` and tree `requirePathTo` throw for absent handles; nullable sibling queries retain their documented absence results. Null and undefined remain valid vertex IDs, never missing-identity sentinels.
